Work queue (in this order, per Andres - new items get appended at the end unless he
says otherwise). Reordered 2026-08-02.

Tier 1 - quick wins (small, isolated changes):

- RESOLVED: session.php polling looked "stuck" on mobile (iOS Safari specifically)
  even at a 1s interval, with updates arriving in bursts and a manual refresh not
  helping either - root cause found and fixed 2026-08-02, confirmed live. Two
  layered issues, in order of how the investigation went:
  (1) A session's sidecar recorded claude_session_id once at spawn and never
  updated it, so after /clear/compact/resume/fork the app kept reading an
  abandoned transcript file forever - fixed via a SessionStart hook, see the
  Tier 3 item below for the full writeup.
  (2) Even once reading the right file, start_app_session() (called by
  session_detail.php/session_history.php/sessions_list.php/quota.php to keep a
  polling tab's CSRF token alive) sets PHP's session-cache-limiter headers
  (Cache-Control: private, max-age=60) - fine for session.php's own HTML page
  (that's deliberately short, see the bfcache fix below), wrong on these JSON
  polling endpoints: it let a browser legally serve a stale cached response to
  the exact same fetch() URL for up to 60s, no matter how fast the page polled -
  and a plain refresh didn't help either, since the reload's own fetch() calls
  hit that same cache entry. iOS Safari does this in practice (confirmed via
  curl showing the leaked header, then fixed); other browsers may or may not,
  depending how aggressively they cache fetch() GETs. Fixed by each of those 4
  endpoints sending its own Cache-Control: no-store right after
  start_app_session(), overriding the session limiter's header.

- RESOLVED: history/prompt bubble text overflow - found and fixed 2026-08-02.
  A real assistant text block in the live homie session (cc-20260802-0522)
  contained "FILTER_FLAG_NO_PRIV_RANGE|FILTER_FLAG_NO_RES_RANGE" (51 chars, no
  spaces) - text-kind blocks rendered with whitespace-pre-wrap but no
  break-words, so a token with no natural break point can't wrap and widens
  the whole page horizontally instead. Confirmed live via Playwright at a
  mobile viewport (390px): scrollWidth was 446px before the fix, exactly 390px
  after. Fixed by adding break-words to the text-block <p> (and the unknown-
  kind fallback <p>) in both render_transcript_block() (PHP) and renderBlock()
  (JS), plus the blocked-prompt card's "Waiting on input: ..." line in
  AgentClient.php/session.php's renderBlockedSection() for the same reason.
  The broader "audit both pages" item below is still open - this fixed the
  two spots actually hit by a real example, not a full sweep.
- RESOLVED: renderBlockedSection() poll-rebuild family of bugs - textarea losing
  focus mid-typing, the page scrolling back to the top while typing, an expanded
  command's own scroll position resetting (read as "only the last few lines are
  visible"), a manually-opened <details> snapping shut. Fixed 2026-08-02, and not
  by trying to carefully preserve each piece of state across every rebuild (tried
  that first for the focus/scroll case specifically, worked but felt like patching
  symptoms one at a time as Andres kept finding more of them) - the actual root
  cause was rebuilding the section's DOM on every single poll even when the
  blocked-prompt data hadn't changed at all, which is the common case for however
  many polls a prompt sits unanswered. renderBlockedSection() now keys off
  [blocked_reason, prompt_context, prompt_options] and skips the rebuild entirely
  when unchanged from what's already rendered - nothing needs preserving if the
  DOM was never touched. The old preserve-and-restore logic (freetext draft/focus/
  cursor, <details> open state + its <pre>'s own scrollTop, page scrollY) is kept
  as a safety net for the one rebuild that can't be avoided (typically just the
  first poll after page load, landing on the same prompt the server already
  rendered), just rarely exercised now. Confirmed live via Playwright: scrolled a
  long command's <pre> to the middle and the page to a mid-scroll position, then
  waited out 3 real poll cycles against unchanged data - both scroll positions and
  the <details> open state were still byte-identical after.
- RESOLVED: "stop Claude mid-response" button - done 2026-08-02. New send_escape()
  host-agent action (mirrors navigate_prompt()'s plain send-keys pattern) + new
  session_escape.php endpoint + a "Stop" button next to the thinking-indicator
  dots (only shown while working). Verified live against a real throwaway session
  (created via create_cc_session(), given a long-form writing task, killed after):
  sent Escape mid-generation, working flipped from true to false within ~1s, and
  the pane showed "Interrupted · What should Claude do instead?" with the
  in-progress text literally cut off mid-word - confirmed to actually interrupt,
  not just a display-layer no-op.
- RESOLVED: truncated command on a pending prompt - found and fixed 2026-08-02.
  parse_blocking_prompt() in Sessions.php used a fixed BLOCKING_PROMPT_CONTEXT_
  WINDOW of 15 pane lines above the choice cursor as the ONLY boundary for how
  much context to show - fine for short prompts, but a command/file-content
  preview taller than that (a multi-line script, a large file being written) had
  its earlier lines silently cut off with no visible sign anything was missing.
  Fixed by finding the real top of the block via Claude Code's own "● " tool-
  invocation marker line (scanning up from the cursor for the nearest one)
  instead - exactly as tall as the actual content, whether 3 lines or 300. The
  fixed window is now only a fallback for the one real prompt shape with no such
  marker (the initial per-folder trust dialog). Verified against a synthetic
  20-line command (deliberately taller than the old window) built from the same
  real-capture format already used elsewhere in the test suite - both its first
  and last lines now come through.
  Live-verified 2026-08-02 against a real throwaway session, with a genuinely
  important caveat found in the process: a real 25-line Write-tool file preview
  STILL came through truncated (starting at line 13) - but this time the ● marker
  fix isn't at fault. Checked tmux capture-pane directly (both the normal call
  and with -S -50 pulling extra scrollback) and got byte-identical output either
  way - lines 1-12 aren't sitting in scrollback waiting to be captured, they were
  never rendered at all. Claude Code's own TUI adapts what it draws to the pane's
  actual height, so a preview taller than the pane simply has its earlier lines
  never printed in the first place - not a parsing/truncation bug on this app's
  side at all, a hard limit of the pane's current row count. A real fix would
  need the pane itself to be taller (e.g. sizing new sessions with a bigger
  default height at creation) - added as its own follow-up item below, separate
  from this one (which fixed the actual originally-reported bug: an arbitrary
  small window truncating content that WAS available in the capture).
- RESOLVED: tmux panes for new sessions needed a taller default height so long
  tool-permission previews (a big Write, a long Bash script) didn't get cut off
  by Claude Code's own TUI adapting to a too-short pane - fixed 2026-08-02 via
  TMUX_PANE_WIDTH/TMUX_PANE_HEIGHT (200x150, configurable) passed as -x/-y on
  new-session. Superseded as the primary fix by the PreToolUse hook below
  (structured, never truncated regardless of pane size), but kept as a
  reasonable fallback for whatever pane-scraping still happens outside a
  blocked-prompt preview. Live-verified together: a real 25-line Write prompt
  rendered in full in the pane AND came through structured via the hook.
- RESOLVED: "Close session" action in the sidebar drawer - done 2026-08-02. A form
  at the bottom of the drawer (below Settings), same POST-to-/ kill mechanism and
  confirm() step as index.php's existing Kill button, styled the same way.
- RESOLVED: tap-anywhere-to-collapse - done 2026-08-02 (first pass had the
  expanded case backwards - taps inside the expanded content were excluded from
  toggling instead of collapsing it, fixed same day). <summary> elements now get
  block+w-full+text-left (so the whole row is a real click target regardless of
  where the actual text glyphs render), plus a delegated click handler covering
  the rest of the <details> box - collapsed OR expanded, tapping anywhere toggles
  it, including inside the expanded <pre>. Excludes taps that land on <summary>
  itself (avoids double-toggling the native behavior) or that land while a real
  text selection is active (so tap-to-dismiss-a-selection doesn't also collapse
  the block) - normal scroll-drag gestures are unaffected either way, since a
  plain click event doesn't fire for those to begin with. Verified live: tapping
  the far-right edge of a short collapsed summary (well past its actual text)
  opens it; tapping inside the expanded content collapses it back; tapping inside
  expanded content WITH an active selection leaves it open; tapping the summary
  itself still toggles correctly either way.
- RESOLVED: images shown inline - done 2026-08-02, tweaked same day after
  feedback. Real captured shape checked first (a browser-automation screenshot
  tool result): a tool_result's content array can carry a text block AND an
  image block side by side - the image block ({type: "image", source: {type:
  "base64", media_type, data}}) was previously silently dropped entirely by
  summarize_content_block()'s default case. Now carried through as an extra
  "image" field on the block (alongside the existing text summary for
  tool_result, or standalone for a top-level image block not nested in a
  tool_result), rendered as a real <img> with a data: URI. A new size cap (8MB
  base64) drops anything pathologically large in favor of a plain text fallback
  rather than embedding it or crashing. Andres's follow-up feedback (same day):
  images are a SIBLING of .tool-detail, not nested inside it - always visible
  regardless of the show/hide-tool-details toggle (a screenshot is often the
  whole point of running the tool, unlike raw text output). Renders as a small
  w-24 h-24 object-cover square thumbnail by default (.transcript-image class),
  tap/click toggles to full size (w-full h-auto object-contain) and back - a
  delegated click handler swaps the classes in place, not a separate lightbox/
  modal. Verified: 5 Transcript.php unit tests (text+image together, top-level
  image, oversized-image fallback), a live Playwright check with a real tiny
  PNG confirming it actually decodes and renders (not just that the <img> tag
  exists in markup). Follow-up bug (caught by Andres immediately, same day):
  the always-visible move above wasn't enough on its own - the entry-tool-
  result-only marker (which hides the WHOLE entry bubble, not just
  .tool-detail, once a tool_result has nothing else left to show) didn't
  account for an attached image either, so the whole card - image included -
  was still vanishing. Fixed by excluding "has an image" from that marker
  too. Caught the bug in my own first verification pass because that test
  bypassed the real render_transcript_entry()/renderEntry() entry-wrapper
  logic entirely (hand-built HTML injected directly into #history-list, never
  exercising the actual marker-class code) - redone against the real SSR path
  via a temporary canned-fixture image, confirmed correctly not marked, then
  made a PERMANENT regression test (the "done" canned entry now always
  carries an image) so this can't silently regress again.
- session.php: Andres reports command/prompt-answer text now appears centered
  instead of left-aligned after the no-wrap/scroll change (see below) shipped.
  Checked live: computed text-align is "start" (left) all the way up the ancestor
  chain (<pre>/<details>/wrapping divs) for both the transcript-history path and
  the blocked-prompt-card path, in Chromium via Playwright - could not reproduce.
  Possibly the SAME stale-page-cache bug already fixed below (Andres's browser
  could have been looking at HTML/JS from before some earlier fix) - worth a fresh
  look now that a genuinely fresh load is confirmed working, before assuming it's a
  genuine mobile-Safari-specific rendering quirk.
- RESOLVED: "unexpected response" alert when answering a prompt or sending a
  compose message, worst on the iOS "Add to Home Screen" launch (no refresh
  gesture, can stay resumed in the same JS session for hours). Two real bugs
  found and fixed together: (1) session.php et al sent Cache-Control:
  private, max-age=10800 (PHP's 180-minute session default), so a browser
  could serve a 3-hour-stale copy of the page on a plain navigation - fixed
  via session_cache_expire(1) in Auth.php's start_app_session(), pinning
  max-age to 1 minute while still avoiding Cache-Control: no-store (preserves
  the original bfcache fix for the Home Assistant iOS webview embed). (2) The
  4 read-only polling endpoints (session_detail.php, session_history.php,
  sessions_list.php, quota.php) never called start_app_session(), so a tab
  left open just watching (never sending/answering) could silently outlive
  PHP's session GC - the resulting stale-CSRF-token POST came back 403 with a
  plain-text body ("Rejected: missing or invalid CSRF token."), which
  fetch().then(r => r.json()) can't parse, surfacing as a bare "Unexpected
  response" with no indication it was actually a CSRF/session issue. Fixed by
  having all 4 join the session too, keeping it alive for as long as the page
  is open and polling. Also improved the error message itself either way
  (parseJsonResponse() in session.php/index.php reports the real status code
  + a body snippet instead of a bare "Unexpected response"). Confirmed fixed
  live: Andres deleted and re-added the iOS home-screen icon (forcing a fresh
  load past the stale disk cache) and it now works.
- RESOLVED: pull quota info from the tmux session itself - done 2026-08-02.
  Claude Code's own status line already shows both rate-limit percentages this
  app needs ("... | ctx: 4% | 5h: 51% (1h 53m) | 7d: 40% (5d 8h) ..." - "5h" is
  the rolling 5-hour window, "7d" the weekly one), so get_quota() now tries
  every live managed session's pane first (quota_from_live_pane()/
  parse_quota_from_pane(), a single instant capture-pane call, no subprocess)
  before falling back to the old claude-quota scrape/cache mechanism (only
  reached when no session is currently running, or none has sent a message
  yet - Claude Code doesn't populate the status line's quota fields until
  then). Live-verified: matched a real claude-quota scrape's percentages
  almost exactly at the same moment (51%/41% vs the pane's 51%/40%), and a
  fresh get_quota() call against a live session came back cached:false with
  the pane's exact live numbers in ~20ms - as fast as the cache path, but
  never stale as long as a session is running. Trade-off: loses the
  per-model weekly breakdown (week_&lt;model&gt;) claude-quota's slower
  /usage-panel scrape provides - the status line only ever shows the
  aggregate "5h"/"7d" figures - not pursued further since Andres's ask was
  specifically about speed, and the UI already degrades gracefully when
  only session/week_all are present.
Tier 2 - self-contained features/fixes:

- RESOLVED: dashboard live polling - done 2026-08-02. index.php now polls
  sessions_fragment.php (new endpoint) on the same visibility-gated pattern as
  session.php, sharing its localStorage interval key so a chosen speed carries
  over between pages. Rather than porting the dashboard's fairly involved
  per-row markup (blocked-prompt cards, folder-trust panel, free-text reply,
  Kill forms with CSRF) into JS, the row/list rendering was extracted into
  shared PHP functions (session_row_html()/sessions_list_html()/
  bare_process_row_html()/bare_processes_html()/session_count_label_html() in
  AgentClient.php) that BOTH index.php's SSR page and the new fragment
  endpoint call - one source of truth, no JS/PHP duplication to keep in sync.
  The poll swaps #sessions-container/#bare-container's innerHTML wholesale,
  skipping the replace when the returned HTML string is byte-identical to
  what's already shown (same "don't rebuild on every poll" reasoning as
  renderBlockedSection() on session.php - avoids collapsing an expanded "Show
  last 3 messages" panel or similar for no reason). Live-verified against a
  real throwaway session: watched the dashboard's session count and a new
  row appear with zero manual refresh, and separately confirmed the skip-
  logic itself by instrumenting the container's innerHTML setter - it held
  steady across identical polls and only fired when this very conversation's
  own tracked session (also shown on the same dashboard) picked up new
  transcript content, proving both halves work: skip when nothing changed,
  update when something real does. Also added a per-row "Thinking…" badge
  (dashboard_thinking_indicator_html(), mirrors session.php's
  render_thinking_indicator_html() minus the Stop button, which has nowhere
  to live on a list row) per Andres's follow-up ask once the poll made it
  worth showing live - verified live too: appeared while a throwaway session
  was actively generating a long reply, cleared the moment it finished.
- RESOLVED: new-content marker on session.php - done 2026-08-02. A "New" divider
  is inserted above whatever a poll cycle just appended (markNewContent() in
  session.php), plus each new entry bubble itself gets an indigo box-shadow ring
  (.new-content-highlight) - not just the divider, since Andres flagged live that
  a thin one-line divider alone wasn't a clear enough signal against a long list
  of same-shaped bubbles. Fade is IntersectionObserver-gated, not a blind timer:
  it only starts once the marker actually scrolls into view, plus a fixed 2.5s
  minimum-visible delay after that (NEW_CONTENT_VISIBLE_DELAY_MS) before fading -
  without the delay, the common case (user already at the bottom, maybeAutoScroll()
  keeps them there) would have the marker intersect and start fading the instant
  it's inserted, defeating the point. Real CSS bug caught live during verification
  (Playwright against a real throwaway session, sampling computed styles in a
  tight in-page loop to catch the transient state): the highlight ring initially
  used a plain `el.classList.remove('new-content-highlight')` to fade out, which
  strips the `transition` property at the same instant as `box-shadow`, so it
  snapped off instantly with zero animation instead of fading - fixed by mirroring
  the divider's own (already-correct) two-class pattern: toggle a `.fading`
  modifier that zeroes the box-shadow while the base class (carrying `transition`)
  stays present, only removing both classes after the animation's own duration.
  Re-verified live afterward: box-shadow alpha traced a smooth 0.6 -> 0 curve over
  ~900ms in sync with the divider's opacity fade, not a snap.
- Audit both pages for any long unbroken string (URLs, paths, tokens, etc.) that could
  widen the viewport horizontally - most text already uses truncate/break-all/
  break-words, but sweep for gaps rather than assuming full coverage.
- session.php/index.php: check whether the "Show tool usage details" toggle's label
