  does (hides tool_result blocks via the hide-tool-details CSS class; tool_use blocks
  are unaffected) before renaming, since the current name may already be accurate for
  half of what people expect it to do.
- RESOLVED: filename-first-line for single-file tool summaries - done 2026-08-02.
  Turned out mostly already true: tool_use_primary_arg_keys() already recognized
  `file_path` as a primary arg, so Read/Edit/Write/MultiEdit already led with the
  filename. The actual gap was NotebookEdit, whose path argument is named
  `notebook_path` - not in the list, so it only happened to lead the summary when
  the JSONL transcript's own key order put it first, not because it was treated
  as primary. Added `notebook_path` alongside `file_path`; regression test added
  with the field deliberately NOT first in input order, to prove it's the
  primary-key match doing the work, not accidental key order.
- RESOLVED: a cluster of session.php history/prompt-answer fixes, done 2026-08-02:
  (1) History entries are now colored+labeled by kind, not just entry.role - rose
  "User", emerald "Assistant", sky "Tool call" (tool_use), violet "Tool output"
  (tool_result), since a tool_result entry carries role=user under the hood same as
  a real typed message, and tool_use/tool_result were originally lumped into one
  "tool" color/label before Andres asked for them split apart (also had to move user
  off indigo to rose - indigo sits too close to sky on the color wheel to reliably
  tell apart from tool_use at a glance). (2) A tool_result-only entry (no other
  content) is now hidden entirely once "Show tool usage details" is off, via a new
  entry-tool-result-only marker class + CSS rule - previously left an empty
  role-label-only bubble. (3) tool_use blocks are now collapsed by default
  unconditionally - two separate force-open mechanisms existed (one in
  renderBlock()'s renderCollapsibleBlock() call, a second, independent one in
  applyShowToolDetails() that force-opened every already-rendered tool-use <details>
  whenever the toggle was off) and both had to be removed; the second one is why a
  plain page reload still showed them expanded even after the first fix. (4)
  index.php's "show last 3 messages" now only shows real assistant text replies
  (fetches a bigger page and filters client-side, since a plain limit=3 off the raw
  transcript could come back with zero qualifying entries), is bordered/backgrounded
  per the same color scheme, has a max-h-64 scrollable container, moved to render
  above the blocked-prompt-or-last-message block instead of below it, and the toggle
  button itself is now a real bordered/padded button, not a bare text link. (5) A
  real correctness bug in the optimistic-UI work from earlier this session: pending
  history entries (both compose messages and prompt answers) only ever cleared via
  exact text-content matching against fresh poll data - fine for compose (the sent
  text closely matches its own transcript entry) but broken for prompt answers,
  since Claude Code's actual response to an answered AskUserQuestion is never
  literally the button's label text, so the match could never succeed - found live:
  a submitted answer stayed dimmed "Sending…" forever, even once the prompt had
  genuinely resolved, and only a page reload (which wipes the client-side-only
  pending state, since there's nothing to reconcile it against) made it "disappear".
  Fixed by tying a prompt answer's pending entry directly to the blocked-prompt card
  itself resolving (blocked_reason changing or clearing) instead of content-matching
  - a definitive signal for that specific case. Compose messages still reconcile via
  content-matching (renamed clearPendingEntries() -> reconcilePendingEntries()),
  which remains correct for that case. (6) On mobile, dragging a text-selection
  handle over the transcript was triggering the sidebar swipe-open/close gesture -
  fixed by checking window.getSelection().isCollapsed in the touchstart/touchend
  handlers and bailing out of swipe detection whenever a real selection is active or
  becomes active mid-touch.
- session.php: audit renderStaticInfo()/renderThinkingIndicator()/renderModeToggle()
  for the same "rebuilds on every poll even when nothing changed" issue just fixed
  in renderBlockedSection() (see the Tier 1 RESOLVED entry above) - renderModeToggle()
  in particular rebuilds a <select>, so an open/mid-interaction dropdown could
  plausibly suffer the same kind of disruption. Not confirmed as an actual bug yet,
  just the same risky pattern in three more places.

Tier 3 - bigger or investigative items:

- RESOLVED (likely): Andres's own replies (typed directly into a real Claude Code
  session, not this app's compose box) not showing up on session.php - root cause
  found and fixed 2026-08-02: Claude Code rotates to a brand-new session-id
  transcript file on /clear, /compact, --resume, or --fork-session while staying
  in the same tmux pane, but a session's sidecar (host-agent/lib/Sessions.php)
  recorded claude_session_id exactly once at spawn and never updated it - so the
  app kept reading an abandoned, no-longer-growing transcript forever after any
  of those (confirmed live against this very conversation's own tmux session).
  Fixed by registering Claude Code's own SessionStart hook
  (host-agent/hooks/session_start.php, installed in ~/.claude/settings.json,
  symlinked into ~/dotfiles) to rebind the sidecar's claude_session_id live on
  every session start/clear/compact/resume/fork, plus a dashboard banner+button
  (check_session_hook/install_session_hook actions) if that hook is ever missing
  (e.g. after a fresh machine setup). Caveat: only fixes it going forward - a
  session that already rotated before the hook existed needs a one-time manual
  write_sidecar() rebind (or its next natural /clear/compact) to pick this up.
- index.php: make the whole session-row "card" a link to its transcript page,
  except for known interactive bits (Kill button, show-last-3 toggle, blocked-prompt
  option buttons, etc.) which should keep working normally without navigating away.
- index.php: list all known sessions grouped by project folder (not just currently-
  running ones - scan ~/.claude/projects for past sessions per cwd), and offer to
  restart/reattach a listed-but-not-currently-running one via `claude --resume
  <session-id>` in a fresh tmux pane. Added 2026-08-02 - overlaps with the "unify
  sessions/bare processes" and reptyr items below (all under the general theme of
  "surface every session, not just ones currently live and app-spawned"), but this
  one's specifically about PAST/dormant sessions, not currently-running bare ones -
  worth reconciling into one coherent design rather than three separate features
  before building any of them.
- index.php: unify the "sessions" (cc-* tmux, app-tracked) and "Other claude
  processes" (bare) lists into one - discussed 2026-08-02, deferred (Andres chose
  to keep going on the hook work already in flight rather than tackle this now).
  The SessionStart hook (see above) fires for every claude session on the box
  now, not just ones this app spawned, which makes this newly tractable:
  generalize the hook to register a sidecar for ANY claude session running
  inside a tmux pane - key it off the pane's own tmux session name (via `tmux
  display-message`), not just the app-set CSM_SESSION_NAME env var, which only
  app-spawned sessions have - then fold those into the main list with full
  features (transcript, compose, answer-prompt, mode toggle all work via tmux
  send-keys/capture-pane regardless of who started the pane). Hard boundary: a
  claude process with NO tmux pane at all (bare terminal/SSH, no wrapper) can
  never get send-keys/capture-pane support - at most a read-only transcript
  link - so a small residual "unmanageable" category likely still remains
  after this.
- Research: use reptyr to close the "no tmux pane at all" gap in the item just
  above, instead of leaving it a permanent residual "unmanageable" category.
  reptyr hijacks a running process's controlling terminal via ptrace and moves
  it to a new pty without killing/restarting it - so a bare claude process
  started in a plain terminal/SSH session (no tmux) could be adopted into a
  fresh tracked tmux pane after the fact, then picked up by the generalized
  SessionStart-hook sidecar registration from the item above like any other
  tmux-paned session. Andres's own starting-point sketch (needs review/
  hardening before use, not vetted yet - e.g. the sudo reptyr requirement,
  ptrace_scope implications, and whether this is safe to expose as a one-click
  dashboard action versus a manual host-side escape hatch):
    #!/bin/bash
    if [ "$1" != "--pid" ] || [ -z "$2" ]; then
        echo "Usage: tmuxify --pid <PID>"
        exit 1
    fi
    PID=$2
    if ! kill -0 "$PID" 2>/dev/null; then
        echo "Error: Process ID $PID does not exist."
        exit 1
    fi
    PROC_NAME=$(ps -p "$PID" -o comm= | tr -d '[:space:]')
    SESSION_NAME="tmuxify_${PROC_NAME}_${PID}"
    echo "Targeting process: $PROC_NAME (PID: $PID)"
    # Stop/background the process, then sever shell ownership so it survives
    # this terminal exiting (TSTP+CONT simulates Ctrl+Z then 'bg').
    kill -TSTP "$PID" 2>/dev/null
    kill -CONT "$PID" 2>/dev/null
    disown "$PID" 2>/dev/null
    tmux new-session -d -s "$SESSION_NAME"
    # sudo is used here because ptrace restrictions usually block non-root hijacking.
    tmux send-keys -t "$SESSION_NAME" "sudo reptyr $PID" C-m
    echo "Success! Process $PID moved to tmux session: $SESSION_NAME"
    sleep 1
    exec tmux attach-session -t "$SESSION_NAME"
- Research: investigate whether subagent calls (Agent/Task tool invocations) are
  retrievable from a session's own JSONL transcript, or only exist in a separate
  sub-transcript file - and more generally, survey what other info sits in these
  JSONL files that this app doesn't currently surface (sidechain/isSidechain
  entries, agent_id/agent_type fields, file-history-snapshot diffs, etc.) that
  might be worth rendering on session.php.
- Research: how worthwhile would a refactor into classes be, or even a full move
  to Laravel 13/Livewire 4, versus the current plain-function/plain-PHP-file
  style this whole app is written in. Added 2026-08-02 - a real architecture
  question, not a quick investigation; weigh against how much this app is likely
  to keep growing (it's picked up a lot of surface area this session alone) vs.
  the cost of a rewrite/migration for what's currently a personal single-user
  tool with no other contributors.
- RESOLVED (the specific bug; the general research lead below is still open):
  compose textarea getting pulled back into view via an unwanted auto-scroll
  during a poll, with nothing new typed - done 2026-08-02, matches what Andres
  suspected ("might be a mobile keyboard thing?"). Root cause: isNearBottom()
  used window.innerHeight + window.scrollY, but window.innerHeight doesn't
  shrink when iOS Safari's on-screen keyboard opens - the layout viewport
  stays full-height while the keyboard visually covers the bottom portion, so
  the old calculation could claim "near bottom" even while the compose bar
  was actually hidden behind the keyboard, and maybeAutoScroll() would then
  yank the page back down to that (keyboard-covered) bottom on the next poll.
  Fixed by using window.visualViewport's height/pageTop instead when
  available (it genuinely tracks the visible area, shrinking with the
  keyboard), falling back to the old calculation otherwise. Verified by
  mocking window.visualViewport to a reduced height and confirming
  isNearBottom() (via the go-to-bottom button's driven visibility) correctly
  flips to "not near bottom" even with scrollY/innerHeight unchanged - the
  exact case the old code got wrong. Caveat: this is a well-documented
  platform quirk, not something directly observed on a real device (no iOS
  Safari available to test against directly) - worth Andres confirming on his
  phone. The BROADER research lead below (a full #app-container flex layout
  using visualViewport.height instead of 100vh, for fixed header/footer
  positioning generally, not just this one auto-scroll bug) is still open -
  this fix only addressed the specific reported symptom, not a full layout
  overhaul.
- Research: iOS Safari keyboard display issues, broader than the specific
  auto-scroll bug just fixed above - the visual viewport shrinks when the
  keyboard opens but layout viewport/scroll doesn't always keep up, which
  could still affect fixed header/compose-bar positioning generally. Andres's
  lead (2026-08-02): use window.visualViewport's resize/scroll events to
  dynamically set an #app-container height (flex column, header/footer
  flex-none, main flex-grow overflow-y-auto) to visualViewport.height instead
  of relying on 100vh, plus window.scrollTo(0,0) on each adjustment to stop
  Safari's "ghost scroll". A bigger layout restructuring than the isNearBottom()
  fix above - only worth doing if Andres still sees positioning issues after
  confirming that fix live.
- Research: how would file uploads work (Andres wants to send an image/file
  from his phone to a session, not just receive them from the transcript).
  Needs a real look at the mechanism - tmux send-keys/paste-buffer only moves
  text, so this isn't just "extend the existing compose box"; worth checking
  whether Claude Code's CLI has any file-drop/paste-image support at the
  terminal level to hook into, or whether this needs an entirely different
  delivery path (e.g. writing the file somewhere on disk and referencing its
  path in a message).
- session.php: after answering a prompt that popped up mid-typing (covering
  the compose textarea while it's focused), the compose textarea sometimes
  comes back too short once the prompt clears - text still there but only a
  sliver of it visible, not the normal auto-grown height. Andres currently
  has to navigate to the dashboard and back to get a normal-sized textarea
  again. Likely related to autoGrowCompose() not re-running (or running
  against a stale/zeroed scrollHeight) at the right moment when the compose
  bar's visibility is toggled back on by renderComposeVisibility().
- Research: can a webapp added to the home screen (the PWA path this app
  already targets, see the README's "Home screen bookmark" section) run in
  the background and send a real push notification when a new prompt shows
  up, instead of requiring the tab to be open and polling? Needs a real look
  at what iOS Safari's PWA support actually allows here (Web Push has grown
  real but still-limited support on iOS in recent versions - worth checking
  the exact current constraints, e.g. whether it requires the site to be
  added to the home screen first, any background-run time limits, etc.)
  versus what'd need a completely different mechanism (e.g. a native
  notification triggered some other way, outside the webapp itself).
- session.php: subagent reports (Task/Agent tool_use + its result) should be
